            Periodicity-Based Triage of APT Command-and-Control Beaconing in Network Flows Using Machine Learning
          </h3>
          <p class="featured-context"> <span>University of Nevada, Las Vegas · 2025 – 2026</span> &nbsp;·&nbsp; <a class="link-inline" href="https://oasis.library.unlv.edu/thesesdissertations/" target="_blank"
              rel="noopener">View on UNLV Repository →</a>
          </p>
          <p class="featured-desc">
            Malware beaconing to command-and-control infrastructure often creates <strong>periodic
              callback behavior</strong> in network flow logs, even when payloads are encrypted. This
            research builds a machine-learning pipeline that ranks suspicious communication units
            for analyst review using only flow timestamps, engineering nine periodicity-based
            features, including inter-arrival time regularity, FFT-based spectral concentration,
            autocorrelation, and sub-window stability, and training a supervised Random Forest
            ranker on CTU-13 with controlled synthetic beacon injection. The pipeline is validated
            across five CTU-13 scenarios covering different malware families and C2 styles.
          </p>

